shared-heap refutation of the naive Sum (per NOME):
  every mi_bin_thr_sum row of a dump carries the SAME heap=<ptr> and SAME committed for
  all thr; e.g. w16.r1 dump#1: heap_commit=253123488 for each of 16 thr =>
  Sum_thr committed = 4,049,975,808 >> pwork process commit = 399,638,528.
  The tls dumps are W views of ONE shared heap; Sum over thr counts it W times.
  SLACK_global (mean over thr) is the physical page slack of the process heap.

identity check — census coverage vs heap bin committed (per NOME, decisive for Leijen Q3):
  W=4   vis_model=  63,800,200  heap_commit min..max = 63,745,344 .. 63,804,656  (vis_model/median = 1.0000)
  W=8   vis_model= 126,908,216  heap_commit min..max = 126,728,800 .. 126,812,688  (vis_model/median = 1.0009)
  W=12  vis_model= 190,016,232  heap_commit min..max = 189,690,176 .. 189,778,160  (vis_model/median = 1.0017)
  W=16  vis_model= 253,124,248  heap_commit min..max = 253,113,248 .. 253,199,184  (vis_model/median = 1.0000)
  => vis(W) = slope_vis*W + a_vis coincides with the heap BIN-COMMITTED bytes to <0.5%.
  The census coverage already counts committed bin pages (used + slack). Page slack is
  therefore INSIDE the visible mass, NOT inside the invisible mass = pwork_commit - vis
  (which lives at arena/chunk level and outside mimalloc bins).

